<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">José Clemente Orozco</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1883">1883</a>–<a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">José Clemente Orozco was a Mexican painter, one of the three leading artists of the Mexican mural movement. His frescoes, darker and more critical than those of Rivera and Siqueiros, depict war, revolution and human suffering. Major works include the murals of the Hospicio Cabañas in Guadalajara and The Epic of American Civilization at Dartmouth College in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jose-clemente-orozco.jpg" alt="José Clemente Orozco" width="320" height="371" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Los Angeles Times · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jos%C3%A9_Clemente_Orozco,_c1930.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>José Clemente Orozco was born in 1883 in Zapotlán el Grande, now Ciudad Guzmán, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalisco">Jalisco</a>, the oldest of his siblings. His interest in art began after the family moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> in 1890, partly through watching the satirical illustrator José Guadalupe Posada at work in a shop window on his way to school. Orozco later called this his awakening to the art of painting. He studied art at the Academy of San Carlos from 1906 to 1914. At the age of 21 he lost his left hand in an accident while making fireworks with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a>.</p><p>He worked as an illustrator for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> newspapers and for one of the Constitutionalist armies under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venustiano-carranza">Venustiano Carranza</a>. Influenced by the Symbolism of Dr. Atl, he followed him to Orizaba in 1914. When the revolutionary factions split, he sided with Carranza and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvaro-obregon">Álvaro Obregón</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pancho-villa">Pancho Villa</a> and Emiliano Zapata. The violence he saw marked his life and art.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1916 Orozco went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, disappointed by the reception of his work in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, and returned four years later to work as a cartoonist. His first mural project began in 1923 with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-vasconcelos">José Vasconcelos</a>, at the National Preparatory School in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>. His early panels caused political conflict and he stopped work; some he destroyed himself, others were vandalised by conservative students, and he repainted many after returning in 1926.</p><p>From 1927 to 1934 he lived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. In 1930 he painted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prometheus">Prometheus</a> at Pomona College. Between 1932 and 1934 he painted The Epic of American Civilization at Dartmouth College, covering almost 300 square metres across 24 panels. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> he painted The Catharsis at the Palacio de Bellas Artes in 1934, then frescoes at the Hospicio Cabañas in Guadalajara, which are considered his masterpiece, and later at the Hospital de Jesús. He died in his sleep in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> on 7 September 1949, aged 65, of heart failure.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Orozco was the most complex of the Mexican muralists. His work has a leftist bent, but less than that of the Marxists Rivera and Siqueiros, and he was influenced mostly by Symbolism. Where Rivera praised the glory of the revolution, Orozco was uneasy at its bloody toll. In The Trench, three anonymous men sacrifice themselves before a barricade whose beams recall a cross. In The Trinity a revolutionary leader threatens the very peasants he claims to fight for, while The Banquet of the Rich uses distortion and caricature to attack the decadence of the rich.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “José Clemente Orozco” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The cultural programme of José Vasconcelos, minister of education from 1921, sponsored the murals of Diego Rivera, <b>José Clemente Orozco</b> and David Alfaro Siqueiros, which influenced public art in the United States and Latin America, and promoted a celebration of the country’s Indigenous and mestizo heritage that became…</blockquote><a href="/empire/mexico">Mexico · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Beyond Europe, Diego Rivera, David Siqueiros, <b>José Clemente Orozco</b> and Frida Kahlo made Mexico a centre, Kenzō Tange joined Japanese tradition with modernism, and in anglophone Africa writers such as Christopher Okigbo and Wole Soyinka used modernist ideas of aesthetic autonomy to declare their freedom from colonial bondage.</blockquote><a href="/movement/modernism">Modernism · People</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ciudad Guzmán, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.71,-103.46&amp;year=1925" aria-label="Ciudad Guzmán on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of José Clemente Orozco"><image href="/maps/globes/g270n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3926 5812h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3926 5812h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.71,-103.46&amp;year=1925">What was here in 1925? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
